{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"what","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"What Next.js adds to React","text":"React draws a screen. It has no opinion about how somebody gets to that screen, where the HTML comes from the first time, or what happens if they have a slow phone. Next.js is the answer to all of that: a framework that wraps React and supplies the parts a real website needs. In plain English React is an engine. Next.js is the car built around it — wheels, steering, a chassis and a number plate. You can build your own car around the engine, and for the first month it goes well. Problem React does not solve What Next.js does How does a URL become a screen? Folders under app/ are the routes. No routing configuration exists. What does the first response contain? HTML, rendered on the server, so"},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"routing","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"Routing by folder","text":"In plain English In most frameworks you keep a list somewhere saying this address shows that page . Next.js throws the list away and uses your folders instead: the folder path is the web address. A folder called settings with a page in it is the /settings page. Nothing to register, nothing to keep in sync, and the shape of the site is visible in the file tree. A folder under app/ is a URL segment; a page.tsx inside it makes that URL real. Nothing is registered anywhere. The file tree is the routing table, which means you can read the whole site’s map with ls . tsx This product, abbreviated Figure How one URL assembles: every layout on the path wraps the page The filenames that mean something"},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"server-client","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"Server Components vs Client Components","text":"In plain English There are two places your code can run, and the difference decides most of what you are able to do. On the server , the code runs once on a machine you control, before the page is sent out. So it can read the database and hold secrets. But it cannot react to a click, because by the time the user clicks anything, that code has already finished and gone. In the browser , the code is sent to the visitor and runs while they are using the page. So it can respond to clicks and everything it knows is visible to anybody. This is the idea that makes the App Router different from everything before it, and the one worth reading twice. By default, your components run on the server only."},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"layouts","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"Layouts, loading and error states","text":"In plain English Three things every screen needs and nobody enjoys wiring up by hand: the frame that stays put while the middle changes, what to show while the data is still coming, and what to show when it does not come at all. Next.js makes each a file with a fixed name, which means they are hard to forget. You read an API key in a page and pass it to a chart component marked 'use client'. Who can see the key? Answer Everybody. The value is serialised into the HTML so the browser can hand it to the component when it hydrates — it is visible in view-source and in the network tab. The file it was read in makes no difference; the prop crossing the boundary is what matters. The fix is to keep "},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"dynamic","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"Dynamic routes","text":"In plain English You cannot make a folder per customer. A dynamic route is a folder with a blank in the name — [projectId] — that matches anything in that position and hands you what it matched. One folder serves every project, and the part that varies arrives as a value. A folder in square brackets matches anything and hands you the value. One file serves every user, every project, every template. tsx app/admin/users/[userId]/page.tsx Pattern Matches You get [id] /users/abc { id: 'abc' } [...slug] /docs/a/b/c { slug: ['a','b','c'] } [[...slug]] /docs and /docs/a/b The same, but the segment is optional. i On Next 15 this changes. params and searchParams become promises: const { userId } = aw"},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"rendering","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"When a page is built","text":"In plain English The real question is when the page gets built, and there are only three answers: when you deploy, when someone asks for it, or in the browser afterwards. Built at deploy time is fastest and can be stale. Built per request is always current and costs a wait. The right answer differs per page, which is why this is a choice and not a setting. Flow Choosing where a page is rendered Replay 1 Same for everyone? Marketing, docs, terms. 2 Build it once Static. Served from a cache, as fast as the web gets. 3 Changes on a schedule? A blog index, a public listing. 4 Rebuild on a timer ISR: revalidate = 3600. 5 Different per person? A dashboard, anything signed in. 6 Render per request "},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"data","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"Fetching and caching","text":"In plain English A cache is a note saying I already looked this up, here is the answer . It makes things fast and it makes them wrong, and the whole craft is deciding how long each answer stays trustworthy. A product price might be fine for an hour; an account balance is not fine for a second. Next.js caches aggressively by default, so most caching bugs here are stale data rather than slow pages. In a Server Component you fetch data by awaiting it. There is no useEffect , no loading state, no waterfall of requests from the browser — the component is already running on a server that can talk to the database. js Server Component fetching In a Client Component you fetch the way the React note d"},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"api","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"Route handlers and middleware","text":"In plain English Sometimes you do not want a page, you want an answer — a URL that returns data rather than something to look at. That is a route handler. It is the same idea as an Express endpoint living inside your Next.js app, and it is how a webhook from another service, or a form submitted by your own browser code, gets handled. A route.ts file is an HTTP endpoint: you export a function per method, and it runs on the server. ts app/api/health/route.ts middleware.ts at the project root runs before a matching request reaches any route — the place for redirects, locale switching and coarse auth gates. It runs on the Edge runtime, which is fast and narrow: no Node APIs, no database clients,"},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"seo","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"Images, fonts and page titles","text":"In plain English This section is the difference between a site that works and a site that feels professional. An image that loads at the right size instead of a four-megabyte original, a font that does not make the text jump when it arrives, and a page title and preview card that look deliberate when the link is shared. None of it is hard; all of it is noticed when it is missing. next/image — resizes, converts to modern formats, and reserves the space so the page does not jump while it loads. A raw img of a 4 MB photo is one of the most common real-world causes of a slow page. next/font — downloads the font at build time and serves it from your own domain. No request to a font host at page l"},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"env","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"Environment variables and deploying","text":"In plain English Some values differ between your laptop and the live site — a database address, an API key. They live outside the code in environment variables, so the same code runs in both places. The rule that matters: anything the browser can see is public. In Next.js, a variable is only sent to the browser if its name starts with NEXT_PUBLIC_ , which makes that prefix a decision rather than a convention. Environment variables keep configuration out of the code. In Next there is one rule that matters more than all the others: ! NEXT_PUBLIC_ means public. A variable with that prefix is baked into the JavaScript sent to every browser. Anything without it stays on the server. A secret given"},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"mistakes","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"The mistakes everybody makes","text":"In plain English Almost every one of these is the same misunderstanding wearing a different hat: forgetting which side of the server/browser line a piece of code is on. Once that line is genuinely clear, most of this list stops happening to you. Symptom Cause Fix useState throws “only works in a Client Component”. The file is a Server Component — the default. Add 'use client' , to the smallest file that needs it. The whole app is in the browser bundle. 'use client' sits at the top of a page or layout. Move it down to the interactive leaf component. window is not defined during the build. Browser APIs touched while rendering on the server. Read them inside useEffect , which only runs in the b"},
{"slug":"nextjs","topic":"here","note":"Next.js","noteTitle":"Next.js","section":"Next.js in this product","text":"In plain English The general version above, now pointed at real files. Open them alongside this — the fastest way to make a framework stop feeling abstract is to see its ideas in a codebase you can change. app/admin/layout.tsx — nine words of routing that protect every admin screen at once. app/admin/users/[userId]/page.tsx — a dynamic segment feeding a detail screen. next.config.js — image hosts, redirects and headers, all commented. services/api.ts — the single place the frontend talks to the Express API, including how the token is attached. Practice Add app/admin/learning/scratch/page.tsx exporting a component that renders your name. Visit /admin/learning/scratch . No configuration — that"}
